WEBVTT

00:00:02.025 --> 00:00:25.134
All right, recording in progress, and I want to welcome you to this week's special event, which is a movie analysis, which I haven't done in a long time. And it's about the movie American Psycho. I would say cult classic, pretty famous movie by now, even though when it came out, almost no one wants to— wanted to see it. Based on a book.

00:00:26.185 --> 00:00:50.068
Which also almost no one wanted to read, which was banned in Germany, but it came out and stayed for a long time. I think 8 or 9 years until it got released. But there was really a time in the German media landscape where a lot of things were banned. Movies, songs, also video games, and of course books.

00:00:51.055 --> 00:01:13.932
So I will look at the movie through a Jungian lens, but this does not mean this is a Jungian movie in the sense of somebody trying to pack in as many Jungian concepts as possible, but rather it is a popular movie. It's a story, and it also resonates with people, and this is already a sign that it likely is saying something about the human condition.

00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:27.123
So for Jung, you have this layer deep in the psyche of each human being. Past and present, which is the collective unconscious where culture happens, creative stuff happens, but where we find also very deep meaning.

00:01:27.493 --> 00:01:49.816
So when you have something that speaks to a lot of people, very likely it's touching the collective unconscious part in our psyche and expresses something or speaks to something that we can otherwise not express. I have to say, I went way overboard with this. I've invested a lot of time for the preparation. Stefan helped me for that really a lot.

00:01:51.758 --> 00:02:09.903
So yeah, this was really a lot of effort. I'm really excited to tell you about all the things that I found. It's, for me, it was very hard to watch again. I watched it only 2 times, one time many years ago, and as a preparation for this event.

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:33.282
And it's hard to watch because there's so many memes about the movie, and I had to think of all of them, of the scenes, of the business card, and just expressions, facial expressions he made. It's crazy how much it is in popular culture. Like if you're on Instagram or somewhere else, yeah, you are confronted with Patrick Bateman really, really a lot. Yeah.

00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:59.686
I did not think I would talk about this movie and make a movie analysis. And this was until I had a discussion with Stefan and because of the very, very, very last scene of the movie, which is the monologue by Bateman. Which I want to recite here. There are no more barriers to cross. All I have in common with the uncontrollable and the insane is the vicious and the evil.

00:03:00.297 --> 00:03:24.063
All the mayhem I have caused and my utter indifference toward it, I have now surpassed. My pain is constant and sharp, and I do not hope for a better world for anyone. In fact, I want my pain to be inflicted on others. I want no one to escape. But even after admitting this, there is no catharsis. My punishment continues to elude me, and I gain no deeper knowledge of myself.

00:03:24.673 --> 00:03:46.114
No new knowledge can be extracted from my telling. This confession has meant nothing. And this is so cool because like all the quotes I have are connected to this one, even by words. And the most important part is the last words he's uttering. This confession has meant nothing. And this is verbatim from the book.

00:03:46.163 --> 00:04:05.679
Normally when you use something from a book and put it in a movie, it has to be streamlined because the book has way, way, way more content and the movie is a shorter experience and more cinematic experience. So everything gets condensed, but there are many dialogues and monologues from the movie that they just took one by one. This is one.

00:04:06.178 --> 00:04:27.386
And now I have a quote by Jung, which is the reason why I wanted them to talk about this movie. And it's about isolation. This is the stuff that drives people crazy because they can't catch themselves anymore. They can no longer control themselves when they have that animal psychology. It means they are driven by instinct. They see a thing and leap for it.

00:04:27.868 --> 00:04:48.471
That is the condition of schizophrenia, for all those things appear also in cases of insanity. They are dynamite that can easily explode in the human mind. If you are caught unaware by these contents, then you can't explain yourself to people. And if you can't explain yourself anymore to your fellow beings, you are in no way different from a crazy person.

00:04:49.186 --> 00:04:58.285
So this exactly describes the situation that Patrick Bateman is in at the end of the movie. And what I will talk about in this event is how did he get there?

00:04:58.544 --> 00:05:17.668
Like, if you would look at it through a Jungian lens, through a Jungian understanding of psychology, How did he end up in the situation that he can talk to no one, feels completely isolated, and had all these experiences that might be or not be true? And for that, it's pretty important to understand Bateman's character arc.

00:05:18.331 --> 00:05:39.579
So in every story, every movie, the character has an arc, which means he's changing from beginning of the movie to end of the movie. There are several transformations happening. That changed the fundamentals of the character, that changed their life. And the movie, the story is like the most important thing that happened in their life.

00:05:39.644 --> 00:05:55.588
This is why the audience is seeing this part. And a little bit like in alchemy where lead is turned into gold through transformation, through the alchemical process. Normally in stories, we see that like a normal everyday man.

00:05:56.969 --> 00:06:15.337
Like Harry Potter or Neo or Luke Skywalker become a hero through the trials and tribulations they have through their struggles. But Bateman does not have that. He does not have catharsis. And catharsis comes from Greek cinema, meaning emotional release.

00:06:15.787 --> 00:06:37.169
So for the Greek, already theater and watching other people struggle through difficulties and overcoming them had an emotion-releasing effect on the audience. This is why people went to the theater. This was why theater was written. So it is a psychological experience, which is very much missing in this story, in this movie.

00:06:38.405 --> 00:07:03.303
And the arc and the central question for Patrick Bateman is really what makes Patrick Bateman Patrick Bateman? Because he has this monologue where he's describing his morning routine. And he describes all the things he does, but they're all superficial. And he says, ah, you might feel my, the warmth of my flesh and my, see my eyes, but there's nothing there.

00:07:03.352 --> 00:07:23.109
This is just Patrick Bateman is an abstraction, an idea. There's nothing real about him. There's nothing that is human. This is just an act, a role that he plays. And he's saying that, he's saying that explicitly to his fiancée that he wants to fit in. And this is why he's doing things.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:48.283
So he's there to create an image about himself and he's willing to bend everything like himself, his body, his personality, the things he's saying, and also people around him just to create this image and idea about Patrick Bateman that people are Yeah, inspired by, afraid of, and yeah, attracted to.

00:07:50.014 --> 00:08:06.396
And this is super interesting to see and funny because he gets challenged constantly throughout the movie. Like the things that people say always push into his inferiority. So he goes through life, he wants to achieve a thing.

00:08:07.646 --> 00:08:30.039
And what we see in the movie is every time when he gets challenged by someone, his reaction is automatically one of aggression and murder. Like someone has a better business card, so he goes out to kill that person. He gets denied at the club that he can't use a coupon and immediately says like, yeah, I want to play in your blood.

00:08:31.529 --> 00:08:50.490
And so everything that agitates him, he has this completely overblown reaction. And this continues through the movie. This is most of the movie. So the movie is just this repetition of he gets challenged and has an outburst and this repeats, repeats, and repeats and escalates throughout the movie. But there's something that's changing.

00:08:52.176 --> 00:09:14.314
And this you have in a story, like there's a, you have a shift where the direction of the movie is going through. And this is the midpoint of the movie very often. And this is when He wants to kill Luis in the bathroom, like he wants to strangle him. And Luis thinks like Patrick tries to approach him and kisses him on his hands. And this freaks Patrick completely out.

00:09:14.362 --> 00:09:36.947
He's completely disturbed, and so much that he washes his hands with his gloves still on and has to return some videotapes, as he says. And this changes something in his psychology. Suddenly he gets more and more agitated. He becomes way more aggressive, but also becomes more unsure of himself and stumbles upon himself and can't speak clearly.

00:09:37.576 --> 00:09:59.129
And his action become more brutal. He's killing more brutally. He's killing more people, but at the same time, he becomes more conscious of his actions up to the point where he has this rampage through the city. And he escapes to his office to call his lawyer to basically confess all the horrible things he has done.

00:10:00.496 --> 00:10:23.642
And after that, he really makes an effort to connect to people. He really tries to connect to people. He talks with his fiancée and says that he's killing people and he has murderous urges and she does not listen to him. And he's saying that she's not so important to him. And she cries, and then he has to again leave and return some videotapes.

00:10:25.135 --> 00:10:42.541
And this culminates in the situation where he confronts his lawyer and tells him all these things. And the lawyer says, oh, this is funny. That's so funny what you told me on the phone, and I could not believe it, and all these different things. And he really tries to make an effort to connect to another human being on a human level, even touching him.

00:10:45.062 --> 00:11:04.899
And the guy does not believe him at all and says like, yeah, what you say is not true. And also stop saying this because it's not funny anymore. And he does not even believe him that he's Patrick Bateman. And this is like this important thing that he says like, I am not Davis, I'm Patrick Bateman. So he states who he is and he's not believed. Like his actions are not believed.

00:11:05.299 --> 00:11:28.644
His person is not believed. His identity is not believed. Like the things that he actually wanted in the beginning, To have this completely malleable thing of identity that he plays around by pretending to be different people. He pretends to be Marcus, he pretends to be Paul Allen. In the end, he says like, no, but deep inside, like, I'm Patrick Bateman. This is who I am.

00:11:28.854 --> 00:11:49.382
And he's not believed. And this is the point where he completely breaks because he realizes that he cannot make a single human connection with anyone. That he has these weird experiences that don't make sense, that likely happened in his head a lot, which the movie is vague about and the book is even more vague about.

00:11:50.783 --> 00:12:09.413
But this is the point where he can't connect and where he's isolated, and this is where he goes mad. And this is where the Jung's idea about isolation comes from. Jung was a therapist, so he treated a lot of people who had visions, hallucinations, fantasies that were, let's say, abnormal.

00:12:10.343 --> 00:12:30.292
He treated psychotic people, schizophrenic people, and he said those people are overwhelmed by the contents of the unconscious. So from the dark parts of the psyches, sometimes images, voices, situations come up that overwhelm the person. That might even seem completely real, the same way as dreams seem real to us.

00:12:30.967 --> 00:12:53.655
And he said their problem is that they feel completely removed from humanity. They don't feel that they belong to the human race anymore. And there you said that's an important function of a therapist to listen to the people, sit down with the people non-judgmentally and just listen to them so that they can unburden themselves, that they can confess.

00:12:54.907 --> 00:13:07.923
This is something that he said that that got really lost even in religion. Like in the Catholic Church, we have this idea of confession and getting absolution. But in the Protestant Church, this is already not present anymore.

00:13:07.988 --> 00:13:32.226
And like today, there's not real confession anymore, except for sometimes in late-night radio, people call just to tell about their weird lives and experiences. And for Jung, this is really a form of the rebuilding rapport and rejoining humanity again. He had some patients with very strange experiences and he would always listen to it.

00:13:32.274 --> 00:13:52.088
He would always take it serious. And by the way they would tell it, he would know whether he could actually help them. For example, they had a young woman coming to him and she said, Dr. Jung, it might sound crazy, but I'm, I'm 100% convinced. That there's a snake living in my stomach and it's telling me things.

00:13:52.585 --> 00:14:12.852
He said, oh, this is a young woman I can help because she realizes that this is abnormal. There's still a distance to that. She's not completely entrenched in that. So he had therapy with her, talked with her, helped her. This is more difficult when people cannot separate and believe, believe like everything that's coming up from the unconscious.

00:14:13.525 --> 00:14:34.496
And this is when they then slowly are removed from humanity, similar as Patrick Bateman, who just failed to connect and have anything real and human in his environment and is then alone and, yeah, gets into his psychosis. So it's American Psycho, very psychological.

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:54.711
And the image he tries to create of himself Jung describes this as a concept of the persona, the persona being the mask that we have in public and interaction with other people. So this is something that can be actively influenced. And I have a quote for that.

00:14:55.706 --> 00:15:15.457
You put on a certain persona expressed, say, in your clothes or in the expression of your face or the words that you use. You affect a particular kind of language even. You take on certain mannerisms for a certain purpose. You are conscious that all of this is not yourself. But you use it as a sort of means. And this is Patrick Bateman.

00:15:15.796 --> 00:15:38.989
Like, everything about him is sculpted, modulated, modified, changed to radiate a certain image of status, power, success, attractiveness. And he talks about this and the clothes he wears. So it's— but this is all superficial. This is all between human beings.

00:15:39.482 --> 00:15:56.287
This is a mask that we are having, and where persona is coming from is super interesting. It also comes from Greek theater. Those were the masks that the actors were wearing. And the reason why it's called persona is it comes from personare, which means to speak through.

00:15:56.978 --> 00:16:13.502
In these masks, there was a little mouthpiece that would amplify the voice because back then, no microphones, no loudspeakers. So they had to work with acoustics of the theater. And also with this little mouthpiece to amplify the voice. And this mouthpiece became associated with the mask that the actors were wearing.

00:16:14.657 --> 00:16:38.595
This then got associated with this abstract idea of having an identity in interpersonal relationships. So what, what we are doing today on social media, for example, maybe dating apps or Facebook or practically everywhere, LinkedIn, we're trying to create a persona. We're trying to transport certain things about our personality and other things we do not.

00:16:40.205 --> 00:17:00.077
So he engages in a performance, which is then interesting that he uses mirrors so much because he tries to see himself as other people might see him. But also that's the only way how he can perceive himself. He doesn't see any value in himself, but rather in the value how other people perceive him.

00:17:01.933 --> 00:17:21.422
Which is funny because when you look at his colleagues and the people around him, they all wearing the same clothes, they have the same hairstyle, they have the same glasses, they even have the same job. Like everybody is vice president. All the business cards look the same. They work in the same company. Like there is no individuality. There's only collectivism.

00:17:22.208 --> 00:17:43.541
But he's striving for that collectivism. He's striving to become interchangeable and then plays with these different identities assuming of other people. So this is a super typical thing in the movie and theme of mistaken identity. He does it willfully, but also it happens constantly. Like, oh, is that Ivana Trump? Oh, is this Donald Trump? Oh, is this Paul Allen?

00:17:44.523 --> 00:18:01.569
It is all the identities are not completely fixed, but rather played around with. And what Jung writes here about the words that, and mannerisms that people have. His speech is completely unnatural in the movie as he talks.

00:18:02.165 --> 00:18:19.005
Actually, he talks a lot like the waiters do in the movie, the waiters in the restaurants, where he's constantly trying to sell or push something and does it with way too much enthusiasm. It sounds fake. This is even what the actor said, that great Christian Bale, who did a tremendous job to play this character.

00:18:19.652 --> 00:18:36.094
They said when they were on set, they did not understand his character and said like, oh, Christian Bale is a bad actor because he's behaving so weirdly. It become only clear when the movie was finished and cut and everything that this was actually the role. This was intentional.

00:18:36.949 --> 00:19:00.246
This is also how Christian Bale met with the author of the book full in character, full as Patrick Bateman, and it freaked the author out completely. Yeah, so he is pathologically lying, constantly performing, but at the same time, super convincing. Like, people believe him. Nobody questions it except for the detective.

00:19:00.649 --> 00:19:15.249
And he's also not working at all. Like, we never see him working. We never see him actually do anything. He's there to fake plans, to cancel plans, to go to restaurants. And this has a specific reason in his psychology, and this has to do with inferiority.

00:19:17.731 --> 00:19:33.059
And I have two quotes for that: "Sensitiveness is a sure sign of the presence of inferiority." And also, "There are people who have the illusion they are better than other men, assume that they are different as if they had another kind of blood. This is all illusion.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:54.764
Therefore, no individual relation is possible with such people." So he is Obviously, when we watch him, neither smart nor competent nor anything. So there's a very strong reason why he might feel inferior and needs to lie all the time.

00:19:55.422 --> 00:20:07.647
And this sensitiveness, sensitiveness that Jung talks about is extremely strong at him. He's easily agitated and his reactions are completely outblown. Like he's killing people.

00:20:08.353 --> 00:20:26.148
As I said, because they get a reservation in a restaurant he can't get a reservation in, Dorsia, or they have a better business card, or they have an account he does not have, or they have a better apartment or whatever. That's a reason to kill people. And this is because his psyche cannot handle this.

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:48.914
He gets reminded of his own insufficiencies and Jung said that's completely typical. So for example, when somebody has an inferiority complex in regards to their intelligence, he said they will use very complicated words and they will wear glasses and they want to look smart, appear smart.

00:20:48.962 --> 00:21:12.654
So people try to pretend that they're very strong where they're actually the weakest in hopes that, that they do not get attacked there. And throughout the movie, he constantly directly or indirectly gets challenged. For example, when he meets the hooker for the second time and she tells him like, oh yeah, their new apartment is better than the old one.

00:21:12.719 --> 00:21:35.088
And it's actually Paul Allen's apartment that he likes more. And yeah, his assistant, when he goes on their date, when the assistant and he asks her where she wants to go and she said, oh, I want to go to Dorsia. And we know already, like, he can't get to Dorsia. He gets laughed off and he never had a reservation there and he will never get a reservation there.

00:21:35.666 --> 00:21:57.536
We know, like, what's making this with the character and will cause another killing spree. And Carl Gustav Jung gave a name to this inferiority. Jung said all the things we don't want to deal with and the things we don't want to be aware of. This is our shadow, our shadow side, the dark side of the psyche where we hide a lot of things.

00:21:57.843 --> 00:22:20.510
This could be negative emotions, that could be trauma. So things of our life we do not want to take care of. We don't want to think about, we don't want to process, so they stay fresh. But what he says is that those things get projected onto other people. So we see the stuff. In other people we don't want to see in ourselves.

00:22:20.623 --> 00:22:42.540
And this creates an emotional reaction and relationship. And this relationship is determined by hate, really hate. Jung says we hate our shadow, and the people we hate around us very likely tell us about something about ourselves, ourselves where we feel insufficient, inferior, and where we have to deal with that.

00:22:44.996 --> 00:23:02.154
And the shadow can be expressed through figures. For Jung, the shadow is a figure that pops up in the psyche, especially in Dreams. And that could be an animal attacking you or a person chasing you or someone very disagreeable in your, in your thoughts.

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:20.089
And in a movie, which is like played out psychology, this is then expressed By figures. So we encounter a lot of figures that, let's say, trigger the shadow of Patrick Bateman.

00:23:22.629 --> 00:23:45.016
For example, the, the detective, as I said, like the detective, Willem Dafoe, seems to know more than he lets Patrick into. Like he's asking all these weird questions. He's challenging what he's saying, like, oh yeah, you need to go through that restaurant. That's pretty far away. That's on the other side of, of the city.

00:23:45.722 --> 00:24:01.605
And he shows him like the CD of the music he murdered Paul Allen to, like, oh yeah, I just bought this record. It's like this super weird coincidence. And this is for Jung nothing unusual.

00:24:01.686 --> 00:24:22.283
He says our psyche challenges ourselves and showing us disagreeable thoughts, fantasies, images, dreams, visions that force us to confront the shadow, to integrate these parts because they should not be separated. They should be within the person. They should be into, in consciousness, but they are not because the person does not want to deal with them.

00:24:24.160 --> 00:24:48.309
Similar with flamboyant Luis, which is the fiancé of of the girl he's sleeping with. I forgot her name, where he said, oh, that's like the biggest doofus in the city, and like, how, how can that be your fiancée? And like, how he can get also nice business cards? And, and then he went to the toilet and Patrick tries to kill him.

00:24:48.870 --> 00:25:13.014
And this is something triggering in Patrick again, because like, oh, that's the biggest doofus. Of course he hates him because he's like not as manly as he is. He is rather flamboyant and seems to be as maybe incompetent— maybe they're all incompetent— as Patrick is. So this reminds Patrick of his own inferiority and causes then the reaction.

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:28.502
And this is then also very interesting and important from a Jungian sense. Like when Luis kisses him on the hand, This is already symbolically an integration process.

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:51.946
So in Dreams and Stories, in Visions, this bodily contact, for example, sexual interaction, is already a form of integration. It's a form of combining. Suddenly he gets into contact with his shadow, and it is not this hateful relationship, but rather a relationship of love. And affection.

00:25:52.316 --> 00:26:12.266
And this freaks him out because his psyche cannot deal with that, even though that this is what he needs. Like, he needs human connection, and this is the first time in the movie that there's like an earnest human emotion connection, words, and he cannot deal with it because it's all honest, it's all serious, it's all genuine and humane.

00:26:12.362 --> 00:26:34.923
It's like not fake and plastic and abstract. But something, something real. And there's another shadowy figure, and this has to do with the homeless person that he kills very much in the beginning. I think it was his first kill in the movie. And he says these things that are super interesting from a Jungian sense.

00:26:34.972 --> 00:26:56.732
He says like, I have nothing in common with you, which is not true. Like, Patrick Bateman is not especially competent. Like, he got his job through other means, and he could also be broke and he could also be poor. But he says like, oh no, there's no way I could be sitting in the street in the dark without money, being dependent on other people and all these different things.

00:26:57.166 --> 00:27:05.203
And this is already showing the separation from the shadow. He says like, oh no, this is nothing to do with me. I've shared nothing with them. Like, we are not even the same species.

00:27:05.652 --> 00:27:27.971
So he kills it because at the same time it reminds him of himself, what he internally also is, because he's not only this flashy magazine cover model, but he's a real human being with way more depth that at the same he does not want to see. And with the shadow and the dark associated is also the nighttime.

00:27:28.343 --> 00:27:44.198
So the day and night in symbolic sense associated with consciousness, daytime. So the things that we can see, the things we can do, but then also Nighttime, the dark things, like the dangerous time when predators come out. His kills happen at night.

00:27:44.696 --> 00:28:04.782
They're happening in the nighttime of his psyche, in his shadow side, where he's living out his dark urges that he's trying to hide during the daytime. So the time when he does not have control, he has control during the daytime, but does not have control during the nighttime. And this. Comes then out.

00:28:04.862 --> 00:28:22.259
And Jung talks about this nighttime psychology and says, when you have tribal people somewhere in Africa or South America or India, they would not go out at night because he said like, people are different at night. People behave different. Other aspects come out. And this makes them then very dangerous. It's the same time today.

00:28:22.339 --> 00:28:37.265
Like if you walk through a city daytime, everything can seem nice. And if you're in certain parts at night, like it's super dangerous and you will get robbed. Stabbed, killed, or even worse fates. So Jung says, oh, the same thing is happening in the psyche.

00:28:40.143 --> 00:28:54.760
And we go deeper, we get deeper through the psyche, like being consciousness, the uppermost level, with persona being the interpersonal thing, shadow being in the personal psychology. But now we're going really, really deep, and we go to the feminine side. We're going to the anima.

00:28:57.483 --> 00:29:20.132
Jung sees masculine and feminine forces in all human beings, but he says like in men, the masculine force is stronger, in women, the feminine force is stronger, but everybody is a whole human being. So they also have part of the other inside of them. It's not so strong, but it's still there. It also needs to be dealt with. It also needs to be integrated.

00:29:20.684 --> 00:29:43.000
And I have some quotes for that that fit to this movie. The intellect can only dissect and discriminate, and the creative element lies out of reach of the intellect in the unconscious. Also, that is one of the foolish ideas that rich men have. They think that Eros is sex, but not at all. Eros is relatedness. And the last one: Eros is rooted in man's body.

00:29:43.690 --> 00:30:04.876
So this feminine force Jung calls Eros. Uh, which is a Greek term, same as Logos, uh, which is the masculine force. And the Logos is abstract. It's airy. It's, yeah, up in the sky.

00:30:06.440 --> 00:30:22.525
While Eros and femininity is like the earth and it's with a higher degree of complexity. In regards of it's not clear-cut. So what he says Logos is doing, it's discriminating things, it's separating things.

00:30:22.814 --> 00:30:45.823
So when you have Adam, for example, in the paradise created by God, like Adam gives names to the animals. So he is taking this broad term of animals and he's saying like, you are fishes, you are dogs, you are elephants, and so on. This is a form of discrimination. This is separation. Categorization out of an unclear mass.

00:30:47.578 --> 00:31:07.684
And this is what he means when he writes, uh, the intellect can only dissect and discriminate. The creative part, the actually creating part of the psyche and human psychology, he sees on the feminine side. On same as like women can get children, but men can't.

00:31:09.488 --> 00:31:32.603
So the creative ground, the earth, this is the feminine. And it's super interesting when you look at Patrick Bateman, who's completely one-sided. He's completely in his head, nothing deeper in the psyche. He's abstract. He's completely on the Logos side. So what does he do? He discriminates and dissects.

00:31:32.869 --> 00:31:49.076
He discriminates people like He's stupid, he's stupid, he's stupid. And he dissects like the bodies of people. He's cutting things up. At the same time, he's creating nothing, nothing of value. He's not even working at all. This is cut off from him.

00:31:49.589 --> 00:32:14.177
And this is why he feels basically inferior because he has to pretend that he actually does something and has achieved something while doing nothing. And there is nothing in him that can create something. He watches those movie tapes. He watches the porn movie in the movie, and he watches Texas Chainsaw Massacre, and he acts out these things verbatim.

00:32:16.130 --> 00:32:38.638
He hires two hookers, goes with them to bed, films it just like the porn, and does the scenes like the porn. He has no original thought or idea. He runs after the one girl with a chainsaw, just like Chainsaw Massacre. She's also screaming the same way. Nothing original. This is just a recycling of things he saw.

00:32:39.952 --> 00:32:56.519
And when he speaks like a waiter and talks about the music, for example, it always sounds like a review. It always sounds like something he read somewhere. He did not come up with these things. These are not the things he feels. He does not feel anything. He told us that in the beginning.

